{
  "dom_id": "step-t8111",
  "name": "The Quotient of a Negative and a Negative is a Positive",
  "problem": "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14147219/assets/67b30f243ef128aefeae5775929643b957330a7e79114abf83aaae7794a21470.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$",
